2026-10-30 07:36:59 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 11, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so Punger has bought 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards, right? So first, I need to figure out how many cards he has in total. Let me do that calculation. \n\nWell, he bought 60 packs, and each pack has 7 cards. So 60 multiplied by 7. Let me check that... 60 times 7. Hmm, 60 times 7 is equal to... 420? Let me confirm. 60 times 10 is 600, subtract 60 times 3, which is 180, so 600 minus 180 is 420. Yeah, that seems right. So total cards are 420.\n\nNow, he wants to put these cards in special pages. Each page can hold 10 cards. The question is, how many pages does he need? Well, I think this is a division problem. If he divides the total number of cards by the number of cards per page, that gives the number of pages needed. \n\nSo, total cards divided by per page capacity. That would be 420 divided by 10. Let me compute that. 420 divided by 10 is 42. So that would be 42 pages. \n\nWait, let me make sure there's no trick here. Did he buy 60 packs with 7 cards each? So total cards 60*7=420. Pages each hold 10 cards. 420 divided by 10 is indeed 42. Yep, so 42 pages. \n\nIs there another way to approach this problem? Maybe converting the numbers into different form? For example, 60 packs times 7 cards each is... 420. So if I divide by 10, it's still 42. So same result. \n\nI think everything checks out. Maybe the answer is 42 pages.\n\n**Final Answer**\nPuncher needs to buy \\boxed{42} pages.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many pages Punger needs to buy to store his baseball cards, we begin by calculating how many cards he has in total.\n\nPuncher buys 60 packs of baseball cards, each containing 7 cards, so the total number of cards is:\n$$\n60 \\times 7 = 420\n$$\n\nEach page can hold 10 cards, which means he needs to divide the total number of cards by the number of cards per page:\n$$\n\\frac{420}{10} = 42\n$$\n\nTherefore, Punger needs to buy **42 pages** to store his cards.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{42}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 74