ree jars: Jar A + Jar B + Jar C.\n\nSo, total marbles = Jar A + Jar B + Jar C\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\nTotal marbles = 28 + 40 + 80. Let me calculate that. 28 plus 40 is 68, and 68 plus 80 is 148. Therefore, the total number of marbles is 148.\n\nWait, let me verify again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Starting with Jar A: 28. Jar B is 12 more, so 28 + 12 = 40. Then, twice as many as Jar B would be 2*40 = 80. Adding all three together: 28 + 40 is 68, plus 80 is indeed 148. Yeah, that checks out.\n\nIs there any possibility I mixed up the operations somewhere? Let me check once more. The problem says Jar C has twice as many marbles as Jar B. So, yes, saving Jar B's amount twice is correct. The addition seems straightforward. Maybe I can check with another method. Let me add all marbles just to confirm.\n\nJar A: 28\n\nJar B: 40\n\nJar C: 80\n\nTotal: 28 + 40 + 80 = 148. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of the problem in terms of equations:\n\nLet me assign variables:\n\nLet J_A = 28.\n\nJar B = J_A + 12 = 28 + 12 = 40.\n\nJar C = 2 * B = 2 * 40 = 80.\n\nTotal marbles = J_A + J_B + J_C = 28 + 40 + 80 = 148. \n\nAll different ways of breaking down the problem lead to the same conclusion. So I think that's solid. \n\nAnother way to think about it: How many marbles are in Jar A? 28. Jar B is 12 more, so 28 + 12. Jar C is twice that, so 2*(28 + 12). Then total marbles would be 28 + (2*(28 + 12)) + ... Wait, but that would complicate. Let me see:\n\nAlternatively, total marbles = J_A + (J_A + 12) + 2*(J_A + 12) = J_A + J_A + 12 + 2J_A + 24 = 4J_A + 36. Plugging J_A = 28:\n\n4*28 + 36 = 112 + 36 = 148. Same answer again. \n\nSo that's another algebraic check to be sure. All methods lead to the total of 148. Therefore, I can be confident that the answer is 148.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of marbles is \\boxed{148}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by identifying the number of marbles in each jar and then summing them to find the total number of marbles.\n\n1. **Jar A** has 28 marbles.\n\n2. **Jar B** has 12 more marbles than Jar A.  \n   So,  \n   $$\n   \\text{Jar B} = 28 + 12 = 40 \\text{ marbles}\n   $$\n\n3. **Jar C** has twice as many marbles as Jar B.  \n   So,  \n   $$\n   \\text{Jar C} = 2 \\times 40 = 80 \\text{ marbles}\n   $$\n\n4. **Total marbles** are the sum of the marbles in all three jars:\n   $$\n   \\text{Total marbles} = 28 + 40 + 80 = 148\n   $$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**  \nThe total number of marbles is \\boxed{148}.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jar A has 28 marbles. Jar B has 12 more marbles than jar A. Jar C has twice as many marbles as jar B. How many marbles are there altogether?", "answer": "Jar B has 28 + 12 = <<28+12=40>>40 marbles.\nJar C has 2 x 40 = <<2*40=80>>80 marbles.\nTherefore, there are 28 + 40 + 80 = <<28+40+80=148>>148 marbles altogether.\n#### 148", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jar A has 28 marbles. Jar B has 12 more marbles than jar A. Jar C has twice as many marbles as jar B. How many marbles are there altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["148"], "solution": "148"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30551, 362, 702, 220, 17, 23, 3594, 38763, 13, 30551, 425, 702, 220, 16, 17, 803, 3594, 38763, 1091, 29595, 362, 13, 30551, 356, 702, 10917, 438, 1657, 3594, 38763, 438, 29595, 425, 13, 2585, 1657, 3594], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 358, 1184, 311, 11625, 419, 3491, 911, 3594, 38763, 304, 2326, 60011, 13, 6771, 752, 1349, 432, 1549, 15516, 13, 30551, 362, 702, 220, 17, 23, 3594, 38763, 13, 30551, 425, 702, 220, 16, 17, 803, 3594, 38763, 1091, 29595, 362, 13, 30551, 356, 702, 10917, 438, 1657, 3594, 38763, 438, 29595, 425, 13, 576, 3405, 374, 10161, 1246]}
