heir capacities together. So, 3 + 3 = 6, total bags 30, so 5 trips. The answer should be 5. So, boxed, it would be \\boxed{5}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many trips Elysse and her brother will need to carry 30 bags of groceries, we begin by analyzing their individual capacity:\n\n- Each trip, both Elysse and her brother can carry **3 bags**.\n- Therefore, together, they can carry **6 bags per trip**.\n\nTo find the number of trips needed to carry 30 bags:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30 \\text{ bags}}{6 \\text{ bags per trip}} = 5 \\text{ trips}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{5}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Elysse can carry 3 bags of groceries into her home with each trip from the car. Her brother can carry the same amount. How many trips will it take them to carry 30 bags of groceries?", "answer": "Elysse and her brother can carry 3 x 2 = <<3*2=6>>6 bags of groceries with each trip from the car.\nTherefore, it will take both of them 30/6 = <<30/6=5>>5 trips.\n#### 5", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Elysse can carry 3 bags of groceries into her home with each trip from the car. Her brother can carry the same amount. How many trips will it take them to carry 30 bags of groceries?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["5"], "solution": "5"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 468, 60392, 325, 646, 6777, 220, 18, 17899, 315, 65408, 1119, 1059, 2114, 448, 1817, 8411, 504, 279, 1803, 13, 6252, 10641, 646, 6777, 279, 1852, 3311, 13, 2585, 1657, 22466, 686, 432, 1896, 1105, 311, 6777], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 468, 60392, 325, 323, 1059, 10641, 1817, 646, 6777, 220, 18, 17899, 817, 8411, 13, 2055, 11, 1156, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 3039, 807, 15963, 862, 22466, 311, 6777, 2