if she already had 15kg before the first purchase, then after buying those two, it's 15 + 15 + 10 = 40. Which again leads me to 15 kg. \n\nSo, I think the answer is 15kg. But just to be sure, maybe I should write down the equations again. Let me denote the initial amount as x. Then:\n\nx + 15 + 10 = 40\n\nWhich simplifies to x + 25 = 40 => x = 15. Yep. That seems correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. The key was setting up the equation properly, correctly adding the two new purchases and then subtracting from the total to find the initial amount. No need for more complicated steps. The answer should be 15 kg.\n</think>\n\nElise buys a 15kg bag and another 10kg bag, and after these purchases, she has a total of 40kg of dog food. Let the amount of dog food Elise already had before buying the two new bags be $ x $. \n\nWe can set up the equation as:\n\n$$\nx + 15 + 10 = 40\n$$\n\nCombining the constants:\n\n$$\nx + 25 = 40\n$$\n\nSubtracting 25 from both sides:\n\n$$\nx = 40 - 25 = 15\n$$\n\nThus, the amount of dog food Elise already had was $\\boxed{15}$ kg.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Elise is buying more dog food. She buys a 15kg bag then another 10kg bag, and she now has 40kg of dog food. How many kilograms of dog food did Elise already have?", "answer": "Elise has bought a total of 15 kg + 10 kg = <<15+10=25>>25kg of dog food.\nThis means she already had 40 kg – 25 kg = <<40-25=15>>15kg of dog food.\n#### 15", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Elise is buying more dog food. She buys a 15kg bag then another 10kg bag, and she now has 40kg of dog food. How many kilograms of dog food did Elise already have?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["15"], "solution": "15"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3984, 1064, 374,