g that extra amount to Cecile's count. Then adding both numbers together gives the total. I think that's it.\n</think>\n\nSince Cecile bought 15 apples, Diane bought 20 more, so she bought $15 + 20 = 35$ apples. The total number of apples they bought together is $15 + 35 = 50$.  \n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{50}$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?", "answer": "Diane bought 15 + 20 = <<15+20=35>>35 apples.\nTherefore, they bought 15 + 35 = <<15+35=50>>50 apples altogether.\n#### 50", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["50"], "solution": "50"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1416, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 11, 1246, 1657, 40676, 1521, 807, 3695, 30055, 5267, 36842, 25, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1597, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 40676, 807, 10788, 30055, 13, 88190, 11, 1077, 752, 1438, 419, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 382, 5338, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 13, 4695]}
