s 0.2 tanks of gas. \n\nWait, but he can't buy a fraction of a tank. So does that mean he needs to round up? Because even if it's 0.2 liters, he needs one tank. So he would need 1 tank. But let me check my math again. \n\nOriginal numbers: 1000 balloons, each 10 liters. Total liters is 1000×10=10,000 liters? Wait, hold on. Wait, I thought 10 balloons × 10 liters is 100 liters. But 10 balloons is 10 liters, and 1000 balloons would be 10×1000=10,000 liters. Wait, so earlier I thought 100 liters, but that was wrong. I made a mistake. \n\nOh wow, that's a big mistake. Let me correct that. Each balloon is 10 liters. 1000 balloons would be 1000×10 liters. Which is 10,000 liters. Because 1000×10 is 10,000. Then total liters is 10,000. \n\nSo then, each tank is 500 liters. So to find the number of tanks needed, I divide 10,000 by 500. Let's do that: 10,000 divided by 500. \n\nWell, 500×20 is 10,000. So, 20 tanks. \n\nSo my initial calculation was wrong when I first thought each balloon was 10 liters. So I miscalculated 10 balloons × 10 liters. I thought each balloon is 10 liters, but if there are 1000 balloons, each holding 10 liters, then total liters is 10×1000. So that's 10,000 liters. \n\nSo that correction makes sense. Therefore, 10,000 liters divided by 500 liters per tank is 20 tanks. Therefore, John needs to buy 20 tanks. \n\nLet me check once more. 500 liters per tank. If he buys 20 tanks, that's 20×500=10,000 liters. Yes, which is exactly the total liters needed. Therefore, the answer is 20 tanks. \n\nSo the confusion was due to a mistake in arithmetic, but correcting that gives the correct answer. Therefore, the answer is 20.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many 500-liter tanks John needs to buy, begin by calculating the **total amount of air** required to fill all 1000 balloons, given that each balloon holds 10 liters:\n\n$$\n1000 \\text{ balloons} \\times 10 \\text{ liters per balloon} = 10,000 \\text{ liters}\n$$\n\nNext, figure out how many tanks are required t