of 5 pages each.\" So, maybe the presses are printing the ads alongside the brochures? Or maybe the brochures are the spreads and also the ads? Wait, the problem says \"the brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each.\" So maybe the total brochures are the prints of both the spreads and the ads? Or maybe the brochures are made by combining the sections? Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, let me read again:\n\n\"A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each.\"\n\nSo, the brochures themselves are the spreads plus ads? Wait, but no. Maybe the brochures are the combination of spreads and ads? Because if they are arranged in any order as long as they make up 5 pages each, so maybe there's a certain number of brochures where each brochu has 5 pages?\n\nBut first, the user is to find out how many brochures the printing press is creating. So, maybe the brochures are the combined pages, regardless of the ads? But the problem mentions that the ads are printed as blocks. Let me try to parse.\n\nLet me think. The problem says:\n\n- Press prints 20 single-page spreads and twice as many double-page spreads. So for the spreads, if they print 20 single, then 40 double. Total pages for spreads: 20*1 + 40*2 = 100 pages.\n\nThen, for each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads each taking 1/4 of a page. So each 4 pages of spreads correspond to 4 ads. So if 4 pages of spreads are printed, that's 4 ads. Therefore, total ads are 100/4 = 25 ads.\n\nSo, total pages printed: 100 (sheets) + 25 (ads) = 125 pages. Then, since brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each, we need to find the total number of brochures. Wait, but how many ads are there? 25 ads. Each ad takes 1/4 of a page. So total pages for ads: 25*(1/4) = 6.25 pages? Wait, but the problem says \"each take up a quarter of a page.\" So maybe each ad is 1/4 of a page. Therefore, the ads account for 25*(1/4) = 6.25 pages. So total pages for brochures is 100 (spreads) + 6.25 (ads) = 106.25 pages. But brochures can be arranged as 5 pages each. Therefore, the number of brochures is 106.25 / 5. But this seems non-integer, which can't be right, because you can't have a fraction of a brochu. Therefore, perhaps my interpretation is wrong.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the brochures can be arranged in any order, meaning that they are all made up of 5 pages each? Therefore, total brochure pages must be a multiple of 5. So, total brochure pages (from spreads and ads) needs to be a multiple of 5. Therefore, total pages is 100 (spreads) + 25*(1/4) (ads) = 106.25 pages. But since this is not a multiple of 5, maybe there's an error in my reasoning.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 25 ads is not 25, but 40. Let me check again. If each 4 pages of spreads print 4 ads. So total pages printed for spreads is 100. Then, each block of 4 pages of spreads corresponds to 4 ads. Therefore, total ads are 100/4 = 25. Yes. But 25 ads would be 25*(1/4) = 6.25 pages. Total pages: 100 + 6.25 = 106.25. So not divisible by 5. Therefore, maybe there is something wrong here. Alternatively, perhaps the brochures can be arranged in any order, but after considering the ads, so maybe there is an extra 5 pages? Wait, but why?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 4 pages of spreads correspond to 4 pages of ads. Wait, let me check the problem statement again: \"For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page.\" So, if the presses print 4 pages for the spreads, then they print 4 ads. Therefore, total ads for the spreads would be 100 pages / 4 pages per ads block? No. Wait, if each block of 4 pages for the spreads is printed as a block of 4 ads, so 4 ads for each 4 pages. Therefore, total number of ads is total pages of spreads / pages per ad. Which is 100 / 4 = 25. Each ad is 1/4 of a page. So 25*(1/4) pages. Therefore, total pages for ads is 6.25. Therefore, total pages is 106.25. Therefore, since the brochure must be made up of 5 pages each, so total pages must be 5 * number of brochures. Therefore, 5 * number_of_brochures = 106.25. Therefore, number_of_brochures = 106.25 /5 = 21.25. Which is not possible", "source_record": {"question": "A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?", "answer": "There are a total of 20 single-page spreads * 2 = <<20*2=40>>40 double-page spreads.\nAs these are made up of 2 pages each, there are 40 double-page spreads * 2 pages = <<40*2=80>>80 pages in the double-page spreads.\nIn total, there are 20 single-pages + 80 double-pages = <<20+80=100>>100 pages in the spreads.\nA block of ads is printed every 4 pages, so there will be 100 pages / 4 pages/block = <<100/4=25>>25 blocks of ads.\nThis is a total of 25 blocks * 4 ads = <<25*4=100>>100 ads.\nAs each ad takes up a quarter of a page, this must create an additional 100 ads * 0.25 = <<100*0.25=25>>25 pages.\nTherefore, the press has printed 100 pages from the spreads + 25 pages of ads = <<100+25=125>>125 pages.\nSince brochures are made up of 5 pages each, this creates a total of 125 pages / 5 = <<125/5=25>>25 brochures.\n#### 25", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["25"], "solution": "25"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 362, 18484, 3493, 374, 18484, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 576, 3493, 23473, 220, 17, 15, 3175, 15112, 42553, 11, 323, 10917, 438, 1657, 1990, 15112, 42553, 13, 1752, 1817, 220, 19, 6816, 16709, 369, 279, 42553, 11], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 2055, 11, 279, 18484, 3493, 374, 18484, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 576, 3491, 33845, 264, 2421, 2155, 5510, 279, 52963, 525, 18484, 279, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 6771, 752, 1438, 432, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 382, 5338, 11, 807, 1173, 220, 17, 15, 3175, 15112, 42553, 13, 5005, 11, 10917, 438]}
