s right. There's no remainder, so there's no leftover desserts. So the answer should be 2. \n\nI should double-check my steps. Total desserts: 14 + 12 = 26. Number of students: 13. Dividing 26 by 13 gives 2. Yep, that seems correct. So the answer is 2.\n</think>\n\nThe teacher brings 14 mini-cupcakes and 12 donut holes for 13 students. To find out how many desserts each student gets, we first compute the total number of desserts:\n\n$$\n14 + 12 = 26\n$$\n\nThen, divide the total number of desserts by the number of students:\n\n$$\n\\frac{26}{13} = 2\n$$\n\nEach student gets **2 desserts**.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "The teacher brings in 14 mini-cupcakes and 12 donut holes for the class. There are 13 students in the class. If each student gets the exact same amount, how many desserts does each student get?", "answer": "There are 26 total desserts because 14 plus 12 equals <<14+12=26>>26.\nEach student gets 2 desserts because 26 divided by 13 equals <<26/13=2>>2.\n#### 2", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: The teacher brings in 14 mini-cupcakes and 12 donut holes for the class. There are 13 students in the class. If each student gets the exact same amount, how many desserts does each student get?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["2"], "solution": "2"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 576, 11079, 12434, 304, 220, 16, 19, 13420, 1786, 454, 61992, 323, 220, 16, 17, 1513, 332, 19766, 369, 279, 536, 13, 2619, 525, 220, 16, 18, 4143, 304, 279, 536, 13, 1416, 1817, 5458, 5221, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 2055, 11, 279, 11079, 12434, 304, 220, 16, 19, 13420, 1786, 454, 61992, 323, 220, 16, 17, 1513, 332, 19766, 369, 220, 16, 18, 4143, 13, 8886