 26 weeks. Then add them together.\n\nYes, that makes sense. Let me write that down. So total miles = (20 miles/week * 26 weeks) + (30 miles/week * 26 weeks).\n\nCalculating each part: for the first half, 20 * 26. Let me compute that. 20 times 26. Well, 20 times 25 is 500, and 20 times 1 is 20, so 500 + 20 is 520. So that's 520 miles. Then the second half: 30 * 26. Let me calculate that. 30 times 20 is 600, plus 30 times 6 is 180, so total is 600 + 180 = 780 miles. \n\nAdding those together: 520 + 780. Let me add them. 500 + 700 = 1200, 20 + 80 = 100, so total is 1300. Therefore, total miles are 1300. \n\nWait, let me just check another way. Maybe breaking down the 26 weeks. So 26 weeks times 20 miles is 520. Then 26 weeks times 30 miles is 780. Total 1300. Yeah, that's the same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe thinking of the entire year as 20 miles plus 30 miles, but then multiplied by the number of weeks. Wait, but that approach might not be correct because you have to multiply by weeks. \n\nYes, because if first half is 26 weeks, so for the first half she ran 20 miles each week * 26 weeks. Then second half is same, but 30 miles. So the total is 20*26 + 30*26. Which is equal to (20 + 30)*26 = 50*26. Which would be 50*26, right? Because 20+30=50. \n\nWait, 50*26 is 50*20 + 50*6 = 1000 + 300 = 1300. Yep, same answer. So that can be a faster way.\n\nSo whether I do 20 weeks *26 +30 weeks*26 or 26 weeks*50, that gives 1300. So the total miles is 1300.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be \\boxed{1300}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many miles Nicki ran for the entire year, we need to break the problem into manageable parts. \n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Weekly Mileage\n\n- In the first half of the year, she ran **20 miles per week**.\n- In the second half of the year, she increased her mileage to **30 miles per week**.\n- The year has **52 weeks** in total, so the first half is **26 weeks** and the second half also contains **26 weeks**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the Miles for Each Half of the Year\n\n- **First half**:  \n  $$\n  20 \\, \\text{miles/week} \\times 26 \\, \\text{weeks} = 520 \\, \\text{miles}\n  $$\n\n- **Second half**:  \n  $$\n  30 \\, \\text{miles/week} \\times 26 \\, \\text{weeks} = 780 \\, \\text{miles}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the Miles from Both Half-Felt\n\n$$\n520 \\, \\text{miles (first half)} + 780 \\, \\text{miles (second half)} = 1300 \\, \\text{miles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1300}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?", "answer": "Each half of the year consists of 52 / 2 = <<52/2=26>>26 weeks.\nFor the first half of the year, Nicki ran a total of 20 * 26 = <<20*26=520>>520 miles.\nFor the second half of the year, Nicki ran a total of 30 * 26 = <<30*26=780>>780 miles.\nFor the entire year, Nicki ran a total of 520 + 780 = <<520+780=1300>>1,300 miles.\n#### 1300", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["1300"], "solution": "1300"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 14991, 72, 7391, 279, 1537, 1042, 4303, 264, 2696, 13, 1752, 279, 1156, 4279, 315, 279, 1042, 11, 1340, 10613, 264, 2790, 315, 220, 17, 15, 8756, 817, 2003, 13, 1752, 279, 2086, 4279, 315, 279, 1042], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 14991