lates to the average number of answers per user. Wait, but how is Q defined?\n\nWait, perhaps Q is the average number of questions per user. So if each member asks an average of Q questions per hour, then the average number of answers is 3Q. Therefore, total answers per day is 24*200*3*Q, and total questions per day is 24*200*3*Q? Then total questions and answers would be 24*200*3*Q + 24*200*3*Q? Wait, but that would be double the original total. Which can't be, because questions and answers are different.\n\nWait, this suggests that maybe there's an error in my reasoning. Let me think again.\n\nLet me define:\n\nLet Q be the number of questions each user asks. Then, since average answers are three times that, average answers per user is 3Q. Therefore, total answers per day is 24 * 200 * 3Q.\n\nTotal questions per day is 24 * 200 * 3Q?\n\nWait, that can't be. Because if each user asks Q questions, then total questions per day would be 24*200*Q. But we just said that total questions per day is also 24*200*3Q. Which would mean that 24*200*Q = 24*200*(3Q). Which would imply Q = 3Q, which is impossible. So there must be a mistake here.\n\nWait so where did I go wrong? Let me check again.\n\nLet me start over.\n\nGiven:\n\nTotal members = 200.\n\nAverage answers per member = 3 * average questions per member.\n\nTherefore, for each member, average answers is 3*(average questions per member).\n\nLet me call average questions per member as Q.\n\nThen average answers per member is 3Q.\n\nTherefore, total answers in all members = 200 * 3Q.\n\nTotal questions in all members = 200 * Q.\n\nBut also, since each member posts 3 questions per hour, total questions per hour is 200 * 3.\n\nTherefore, total questions per day is 200 * 3 * 24 hours.\n\nTotal answers per day is 200 * 3Q as discussed.\n\nTherefore, total number of questions and answers per day is [200 * 3 * 24] + [200 * 3Q].\n\nBut wait, but Q is average questions per member. So how is Q determined?\n\nThe problem does not