 feet, then the number of coats would be ceiling(1600 / 400) = 4. Therefore, yes, paints with two coats would be 4 gallons. So total paint gallons is 4*45=180. Then, since two people, each contributes 90.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem doesn't consider the second coat and just needs a single coat. Let me check the problem statement again: \"will need a second coat.\" So, \"need a second coat\" which probably means they have to apply a second coat. Therefore, they need two coats, each requiring 400 square feet. Which would mean two gallons. Therefore, total cost is 5 gallons. Wait, no. Let me do the math again.\n\nSuppose total area of walls is 1600 square feet. If they need two coats, then total coverage is 2 * 400 = 800 square feet. Therefore, paint required is 800 / 400 =", "source_record": {"question": "Jason and Jeremy want to paint their wall white and agreed to split the cost of the paint. A gallon of paint costs $45 and can cover up to 400 square feet. How much will each of them contribute to the cost of the paint if their walls have a total area of 1600 square feet and will need a second coat?", "answer": "They need 1600 square feet / 400 square feet/gallon = <<1600/400=4>>4 gallons of paint for their walls.\nSince they will do a second coating, then they would need 4 gallons x 2 = <<4*2=8>>8 gallons in all.\nEight gallons of paint will cost $45/gallon x 8 gallons = $<<45*8=360>>360.\nThus, Jason and Jeremy will each contribute $360/2 = $<<360/2=180>>180.\n#### 180", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jason and Jeremy want to paint their wall white and agreed to split the cost of the paint. A gallon of paint costs $45 and can cover up to 400 square feet. How much will each of them contribute to the cost of the paint if their walls have a total area of 1600 square feet and will need a second coat?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["180"], "solution": "180"}, "prompt_to